	<key>readme</key>
	<string># gTasks ✅

Create and manage Google Tasks with Alfred.

More info: https://github.com/giovannicoppola/alfred-gtasks

## Usage
Type `gt_new` (configurable) followed by the task and an optional due date, e.g. `gt_new Call the plumber tomorrow`. The subtitle shows the due date before you press `Enter`.

- `Enter`: create the task
- `⌥`: create the task exactly as typed, with no due date

Type `gt_tasks` (configurable) to list your open tasks. On any result:

- `⌘`: mark as complete
- `⌘⌥`: delete

## Due dates
`today`, `tomorrow`, `friday`, `next friday`, `next week`, `next month`, `in 3 days|weeks|months`, `2026-12-03`, or `12/15` (or `12-15`) as the last word.

Add a time with `:hh:mm`, e.g. `12-15:09:30`. Google Tasks stores only the date, so the time is added to the task title.

## Setting up
gTasks needs your own (free) Google OAuth client:

1. In the [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/), enable the **Google Tasks API**, set up the **OAuth consent screen** (External) and add your Google account as a **test user**.
2. Under **Credentials**, create an **OAuth client ID** of type *Desktop app* and download the JSON.
3. Select that file as **Key File** below.
4. Type `gt_tasks` and press `Enter` on *Sign in to Google*.

While the consent screen is in *Testing*, Google asks you to sign in again every 7 days. Set its publishing status to *In production* to avoid that.

## What's new in 0.2
- 📦 Nothing to install: runs on the macOS system Python
- 🔑 Sign-in problems now show in Alfred, with `Enter` to sign in again
- 📅 Better due-date parsing, and `⌥` to skip it

## Feedback
Bug reports and ideas are welcome on [GitHub](https://github.com/giovannicoppola/alfred-gtasks/issues) or the [Alfred forum](https://www.alfredforum.com/).
</string>
